{% block how_it_works %}
## How it works

A screen of many events splits the users over many edges (or cells), and most of
them are too thin to test: rare events break popular transitions into
fragments, and events that always follow each other split one behaviour into
two edges. `get_ab_compression` proposes a smaller vocabulary for the same
users, one operation at a time, and returns the plan: what to do with every
event, and why. [`compress_events(plan)`](/docs/data-processors/compress-events)
applies it.

### The operations

Each step of the greedy path applies one operation, and the numbers of the
screen after it (cells, testable cells, median MDE, transition mass retained,
artificial transitions) are one row of `attrs["path"]`:

- **merge a chain** `A → B` into `A+B` when `P(next = B | A)` and
  `P(prev = A | B)` both reach `chain_threshold` (pooled): the two events are one
  behaviour. Runs of the merged label collapse into one event, so a merge
  creates no self-loop that its members did not have.
- **drop a rare event** (in fewer than `min_path_share` of the paths) when it
  only interrupts transitions that exist anyway: removing `X` turns `A → X → B`
  into `A → B`, and the screen decides whether that is faithful (below).
- **relabel a rare event to `other`** when dropping it would not be faithful: it
  keeps the event's position and invents no `A → B`.
- **merge synonyms**: two events with the same incoming and outgoing transitions
  (Jensen-Shannon distance at most `synonym_threshold`, no self-loops of their
  own), one of them thin, when the merge leaves fewer untestable cells and loses
  no testable one.

On both screens a drop never invents a transition: when dropping a rare event
would join two events that never follow each other directly, it is relabelled
`other` instead. `keep` events, `path_start` / `path_end` and the events of a
declared `edges=` family are never touched. `step=k` returns the plan at another
step than the chosen one.

### Which step: the information, not the cells

Every step records `untestable_share`: the share of the screen's observations
(each path's incidence on a cell, for `"unique_paths"` edges and step cells; the
transitions, for the count weights) that sit in cells with fewer than
`min_support` paths, too thin to test. The default `target="untestable_share"`
takes the earliest step whose share is within 10 % of the path's lowest (and at
least within 0.01 % of the observations), among the steps without an artificial
transition. Counting cells instead misjudges a chain: merging `A -> B`, where `B`
always follows `A`, removes a testable cell that tells nothing `A` does not, and
a cell count reads that as a loss. A dense screen, whose thin cells hold almost
none of the observations, keeps the stream as it is (step 0); a cell that nearly
every path has fails the support rule on the other side, and no merge can help
it, so it does not count. `target="testable"` (the most testable cells) and
`target="mde"` (the lowest median MDE) remain.

### Under a cap: merge neighbours

With `max_events`, once no operation above applies and the vocabulary is still
above the cap, each step takes the cheapest **merge of neighbours** among three
kinds of candidates: the strongest chain pair left (`min(P(next), P(prev))` of
at least 0.5), the closest synonym pair (Jensen-Shannon distance at most 0.5), and
each of the three rarest events left with each of its three closest neighbours
(the labels next to it in most of its runs, merged as `operation =
"merge_neighbour"`). The cost of a merge is the events it loses: the runs of the
merged label that hide which of the two happened, and the repeats its collapsed
runs erase (a chain whose every run is `A B` costs nothing). A frequent event is
never dropped, and never relabelled `other` (that takes only events below
`min_path_share`), while a merge is left; only when none is, the rarest event is
dropped, with its artificial transitions counted. A cap far below the vocabulary
builds long merged labels, as a merged label is often the closest neighbour of
the next rare event: declare in `keep` the events the decision rests on (the
treatment's and the metric's), and read the labels before applying the plan.

### How much data each vocabulary needs

With `target_delta=` (absolute, in the screen's units), every step gets two more
columns, projected as [`get_ab_transitions_power`](/docs/tools/get-ab-transitions-power)
projects them (the same path composition, more paths): `median_scale_needed`, the
multiple of the current users at which half of the step's testable cells detect
`target_delta`, and `scale_to_test_90`, the multiple at which 90 % of its cells
pass the support rule. Read along the path, they say how much data a screen of
each vocabulary size needs.

### One plan per screen

The two screens need different vocabularies, and `screen` builds the plan for
one of them:

- `"transitions"` wants **fewer, richer edges with no artificial transition**: a
  rare event is dropped only when the events around it already follow each other
  directly in at least `min_support` paths for `bridge_threshold` of its runs,
  else it is relabelled `other`; chains are merged first. Every compressed edge
  that no original direct transition maps to is counted in
  `artificial_transitions`.
- `"steps"` wants **aligned steps**: a rare event is dropped when it is an
  insertion (the paths without it go from the event before to the event after
  directly at least as often as through one other event), because dropping it
  moves the later events of its paths back to the step of the paths without it;
  otherwise it is relabelled `other`, which keeps every position. Rare events
  come first, chains last.

On `load_ab_test()` with `min_path_share=0.1`, `support_chat` (8.5 % of the
users) is rare for both screens. All of its runs sit between two events that
also follow each other directly, so the transitions plan drops it; only 57 % of
them are insertions (the paths without it more often take another event at that
place), so the steps plan relabels it `other` to keep the later steps in place.

### Chosen without the arms

Every statistic that drives the plan is computed on the pooled paths of both
arms: the arm of a path is never read, only the two arm sizes (for the MDE).
Choosing the vocabulary from the arms' differences would be selection on the
outcome, and the screen on the compressed stream would no longer control its
false discoveries; filtering on a statistic that is independent of the arms
under the null, as the pooled support is, keeps Benjamini-Hochberg valid
("independent filtering", Bourgon, Gentleman and Huber, 2010). Permuting the
arm labels, or swapping the arms in `diff`, leaves the plan identical. Fix the
plan before reading the screen it is for, and declare the events the treatment
introduces or changes in `keep`: the plan cannot see the arms, so it cannot tell
a synonym from the treatment's replacement of an event.

### Homogeneity

With `by=<a segment fixed before exposure>`, every segment gets its own plan (and
the segment column on every row), and `attrs["homogeneity"]` a summary per
segment: the effective vocabulary size (`exp` of the entropy of the event
distribution), the transition entropy in bits, and the share of distinct paths.
A homogeneous segment (a few events carry most of the paths, many users share a
path) reaches enough testable cells with little compression; a heterogeneous
one needs more. The segment must be fixed before exposure, as for the
scorecard's strata: pass `by_pre_exposure=True`, or the stream's recipe must
show it.

### On the bundled datasets

The bundled experiments are dense: on every one, the thin cells hold less than
0.01 % of the observations, and the default plans keep the stream as it is. A cap
forces a real compression: on `load_ab_test()`, `max_events=10` with
`keep=["quick_add", "cart"]` merges the checkout chain into
`checkout_start+payment+order_success` and three detours that mostly return
home (`support_chat`, `wishlist_add`, `reviews_view`) into
`reviews_view+wishlist_add+support_chat+home`; nothing is dropped. 60 of the 61
edges of the compressed screen are testable (90 of 92 before), and its 17
discoveries include the planted ones (`cart → cart`, `product_view → quick_add`,
`quick_add → cart`). Compression is not free: the merged runs took 5.1 % of the
transitions with them (`mass_retained` 0.949), and the median MDE of the testable
edges rose from 2.03 to 2.55 pp, as the best-measured detour edges left the
family. Neither the default plans nor these capped ones create an artificial
transition.

On a 40,000-user simulated store with 115 events, the default plan does compress:
on the transition screen it takes step 35 of 38 (332 untestable edges of 1,623
→ 195 of 1,357; the thin edges' share of the observations 0.160 % → 0.112 %), on
the step screen step 22 of 38 (52 untestable cells → 30). With `max_events=40`
both screens reach 40 events with no artificial transition, by merges of
neighbours once nothing rare is left: only events below 1 % of the paths are
dropped or relabelled `other`.

